WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:10.230
Optimization view modes are a really powerful way to get a visualization of

2
00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:16.469
what your scene is doing at any given view type and really powerful to help

3
00:00:16.469 --> 00:00:21.829
support you in your quest for getting optimization. So you can find these

4
00:00:21.829 --> 00:00:24.929
different view modes from your viewport. You can change the drop-down

5
00:00:24.929 --> 00:00:30.550
from the lit model and change the view mode. There's many many different

6
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.549
types that you can pick which we'll cover a few of the the more common

7
00:00:34.549 --> 00:00:38.030
ones here today but it's definitely worth playing around with all the

8
00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.750
different view modes. So again in your viewport where you might see lit or

9
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.590
unlit, the button there at the top of your main viewport, you can

10
00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:52.350
go through the the modes there. So light complexity is quite useful for

11
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.990
knowing how much resource is being used by the lights. This is

12
00:00:55.990 --> 00:01:00.070
especially going to be predominant if you've got a dynamically lit scene for

13
00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:04.049
example or you've got a lot of dynamic lights in your scene when you want the

14
00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:09.730
majority of it to be just statically rendered. So an ideal light complexity is

15
00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:16.409
this black to dark blue. As soon as it starts getting to green and orangey red

16
00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:20.189
we start getting a little bit more expensive as soon as your scene starts

17
00:01:20.189 --> 00:01:25.730
growing purpley pink a lilac color or white that means like white gets

18
00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:31.290
it's almost like white hot in terms of lighting complexity. You really want

19
00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.790
to try and avoid that. That can be caused when there's a lot of dynamic

20
00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:40.010
lights overlapping each other. So yeah lighting will have a big impact

21
00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:44.329
especially if you're deciding on you know the use of real-time global

22
00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:48.650
illumination with lumen or something or whether you want statically baked

23
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.890
scene. Again it depends on the project, it depends on the desired platform, what

24
00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:57.730
can be supported but light complexity is a very useful thing to be able to view

25
00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:05.010
here. We can also view Nanite in the scene as well. So when we look at Nanite

26
00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:11.229
we can see the instances, we can see the different primitives that are used, we

27
00:02:11.229 --> 00:02:16.129
can see the different materials that are used, any overdraw that's going on. So

28
00:02:16.129 --> 00:02:20.330
when we're referring previously to the overdraw of meshes what is unreal

29
00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:26.169
taking a few different passes on rendering. So overdraw can be caused when

30
00:02:26.169 --> 00:02:30.210
it's trying to render something at a distance. So it renders the scene once,

31
00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.969
it's like right I've not got everything, it renders it again, it renders it again

32
00:02:32.969 --> 00:02:37.210
and how many times it needs to render that frame can cause

33
00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:41.490
overdraw. And then you've also got different masks and this is just a

34
00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:48.780
really useful pre-visualization of your Nanite here. And then when

35
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.639
we're talking about Nanite overdraw you want to see these kind of darker

36
00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:59.759
patches in the Nanite overdraw. As soon as it starts getting more layered in its

37
00:02:59.759 --> 00:03:04.879
approach and starts turning a bit more orangey there that's where it gets very

38
00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:09.419
expensive. So the expense starts when the color starts creeping in with the

39
00:03:09.419 --> 00:03:14.479
orange, as soon as you start getting brighter oranges almost like getting

40
00:03:14.479 --> 00:03:20.199
shades of white in there that's where it gets very expensive.
